# New To-do source experiment

This is a proposed, independently reviewed source experiment for a dedicated New to-do draft action. It does not install these changes in the normal Notes application. The application payload is the exact independently reviewed R5 proposal; its source authority and independent review are retained in `review/`. No execution result is represented by this document or `bundle.json`.

The composer authenticates all 32 committed Notes files, the exact before images, the original-source dependencies, shared host inputs and committed workflow before copying seven explicit after images into an ephemeral hosted checkout. Three existing files change and four new tests are added. README is separately pinned; six executable/test targets appear in the complete six-directory active-source census. Every other eligible source is checked against its Git blob. All 36 resulting Notes files and the entire eligible census must remain identical after execution. The existing installed32-source verifier and all historical receipts remain unchanged.

The finite job selects exactly 21 files and requires 111 passed groups, zero failures/cancellations/skips/todos, all 23 previously required names and all 19 added names exactly once. Its three separate comparison buckets are 20 existing conversion/completion helper comparisons, four new parent/type helper comparisons over a disclosed fields adapter, and two desktop-command body/type argument comparisons. These comparisons are not added to test-group counts. The original Joplin desktop application, database, provisional persistence and GUI are not executed. The original desktop command provisionally saves a new note; this candidate deliberately stores nothing until explicit Save.

The eight Chromium scenarios use actual enabled UI controls, native discard dialogs and a real owned loopback host. They cover unsaved creation, genuine title validation, explicit retry, task/ordinary-note draft choice, first-save duplicate prevention, typing after an actual commit, newer editor ownership, navigation disposal, post-commit refresh failures, and actual JEX/resource downloads. Three successful responses and one genuine failed response are held only after the real host has responded. The suite has zero disabled-handler probes, retries or skipped cases.

Two refresh-failure scenarios deliberately create a filesystem read fault inside their own synthetic data directory. The list request is held before forwarding; the prior attachment-list response and every other owned request must finish first. The committed state file is byte-checked and renamed to an owned sibling, an empty directory temporarily occupies its exact path, and the unchanged host naturally returns HTTP400 when reading it. The empty directory is removed without recursion and the exact original file is restored and byte-verified in `finally` before the original response is delivered. This is a synthetic I/O fault, not an ordinary conflict or a simulated HTTP response. Any incidental request failure, incomplete restoration or cleanup rejects acceptance.

Every successful scenario retains a PNG, raw synthetic final state and complete owned cleanup record. The download scenario retains the real browser JEX and resource files, a native Windows TAR listing and a second independent bounded TAR readback. All seven prior imported raw records and the exact five-byte binary must remain identical alongside the new task. Screenshots, expected HTTP400 bodies, requests, responses, timing observations and full child stdout/stderr remain evidence. Traces and videos are disabled.

Both jobs require the pinned Node24.16.0 runtime on standard GitHub-hosted Windows2022/X64 runners with read-only repository credentials. The browser acquisition step uses the unchanged locked Playwright1.64.1 tooling and Chromium156.0.8078.4/revision1248. The workflow preserves complete success or failure evidence with fixed 2,500-file/96MiB raw/8MiB individual/15MiB ZIP limits and one-day artifact retention. Local recoverable backup collection must preserve actual completed-run identities and raw evidence; no source-only declaration can substitute for that acceptance.

The composer’s distinct closed proof group is 60 paths under 64 paths/8MiB. The collector’s tooling group is 12 paths/8MiB, with its own source additionally Git-bound and present in the full active census. Those groups do not expand the historical common159/160, Archive94/96, installed Notes52/64 or publication92/96 limits. The new workflow and every tool must be committed before cloud execution. The collector checks literal frozen after identities; it does not derive expected after bytes from mutable live files. Its own identity is the actual checked-out Git blob, avoiding a self-pin cycle.

This finite experiment does not establish complete Joplin feature parity, original-GUI acceptance, cross-platform behavior, reminder support, browser-matrix coverage, security certification or superiority. Due dates help track tasks; reminders are not available yet. Installation and broader comparisons require distinct future evidence.

The earlier source epoch ran as study38926216286 and failed, with107/111 finite groups passed (4 failed) and7/8 browser cases passed (1 failed). Its complete raw evidence and unchanged source bytes remain historical. R5 corrects notebook-operation admission during pending Save and makes canonical baseline comparisons explicit. Invalid400/GET/restart/list checks preserve exact persisted bytes; a later valid write may materialize existing read normalization. The browser raw-note invariant uses a real second ordinary Save at revision2/history1/metadataEdited:true. This R5 experiment has not executed.
